amic instruction construction is powerful\n        - All operators expect (instruction: str, context: str) except Ensemble which takes contexts: List[str]\n        """\n        import asyncio\n\n        # Step 1: Extract the function name from the test cases\n        func_name = await self.generate(\n            instruction="""\n            Extract the function name from the test cases provided in the problem description.\n            The function name is typically the name of the function being tested in the assert statements.\n            Return ONLY the function name as a plain text string, without any additional explanation or formatting.\n            """,\n            context=self.problem_text\n        )\n\n        # Step 2: Generate the initial implementation of the function\n        initial_code = await self.generate(\n            instruction=f"""\n            Write a Python function named \'{func_name}\' that solves the task described in the problem.\n            Requirements:\n            1. Include ALL necessary import statements at the top.\n            2. Handle edge cases such as empty inputs, None values, and boundary conditions.\n            3. Ensure the function signature matches the test cases provided.\n            4. Return ONLY the executable Python code, without any explanations or additional text.\n            """,\n            context=self.problem_text\n        )\n\n        # Step 3: Revise the initial code to ensure correctness and completeness\n        revised_code = await self.revise(\n            instruction="""\n            Review the provided Python code and ensure it meets the following criteria:\n            1. All necessary import statements are included.\n            2. Edge cases are handled appropriately.\n            3. The function signature matches the test cases.\n            4. The code is clean, efficient, and follows Pythonic best practices.\n            5. Fix any issues such as off-by-one errors, type mismatches, or missi