r boundary conditions.\n            3. Return ONLY the executable Python code, without any explanations or comments.\n            4. Ensure the function signature matches the test cases exactly.\n            """,\n            context=self.problem_text\n        )\n\n        # Step 3: Revise the generated code for correctness and completeness\n        revised_code = await self.revise(\n            instruction="""\n            Review the generated code and make any necessary improvements:\n            1. Ensure all required imports are included.\n            2. Check for edge cases and handle them appropriately.\n            3. Verify that the function signature matches the test cases.\n            4. Fix any syntax errors or logical issues.\n            Return ONLY the corrected Python code, without any explanations or comments.\n            """,\n            context=initial_code\n        )\n\n        # Step 4: Ensemble decision (optional but recommended for complex problems)\n        # If needed, compare multiple candidate solutions here\n        # For simplicity, we\'ll use the revised code as the final output\n        final_code = await self.ensemble(\n            instruction="""\n            Evaluate the following candidate solutions and select the best one:\n            1. Ensure the code is clean, efficient, and follows Python best practices.\n            2. Verify that the function name matches the test cases.\n            3. Confirm that the code passes all test cases.\n            Return ONLY the final, corrected Python code.\n            """,\n            contexts=[initial_code, revised_code]\n        )\n\n        return final_code\n```', '<think>\n</think>\n\n```python\n# --- DO NOT IMPORT HERE ---\nclass Workflow:\n    def __init__(self, config, problem) -> None:\n        # --- DO NOT MODIFY THIS SECTION ---\n        self.config = config\n        self.problem_text = problem\n        self.llm = create(config)\n        \n        self.generate = operator.Gene